s` remains []. End of loop. Save `tags_map[B] = sorted([])`. Correct. The logic appears functionally correct for simple cases. Why did it fail? The plan mentions `list_notes` consumes 0.112s. The patch does not change the `total` count query logic or the `search_text`/`tags_filter` construction significantly, except for the data fetch. The validation failure might stem from the `tags_filter` logic in the JOIN. The `WHERE` clause uses `id IN (SELECT ... GROUP BY ... HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT nt.tag) = ?)`. This subquery is executed for *every* row in the main query if not optimized, or rather, it's part of the WHERE clause. The main query is `SELECT ... FROM notes n LEFT JOIN note_tags nt ... WHERE ...`. If `tags_filter` is present, the subquery filters the `notes` table. The JOIN then attaches tags. This is logically sound. However, the `params` list construction for the subquery uses `params.extend(wanted)` and `params.append(len(wanted))`. The `data_params` uses `params + [limit, offset]`. This shares the list reference? No, `params` is a list, `data_params` is a new list created by concatenation. This is safe. The failure might be due to the `search_text` handling. `conditions.append(\"  \t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t